    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d457-a1">

                <pb facs="0552" n="464" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg464"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Griffith to Mr. Clayton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Matamoros</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-07-29"><hi rendition="#italic">July 29,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to advise you that the
                    case of Harding and Dugat, concerning which I telegraphed you last January, has
                    never been settled. My intercession in their behalf had the effect of a
                    reduction of their bond from $2,000 gold to $300 Mexican and their release, but
                    the case has never been settled. This $300 bond still hangs over them. One of
                    the men is a rice planter near Matamoros, on the Mexican side, and is desirous
                    of visiting his children, near Houston, but can not leave on account of this
                    bond. I take the liberty of suggesting that you write to Lie. José H. Serret,
                    judge of the second district, in Nuevo Laredo, to whose court the case has been
                    transferred, and see if you can obtain any information as to when this case will
                    be settled, or do whatever else you deem proper.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. Merrill
                    Griffith.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
